 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Adds a linear cost relative to the image size for each blurred pixel per iteration. The cost is $O(H \cdot W \cdot k^2)$ per image per iteration, where $k$ is the kernel size [G]. Since the kernel size decreases, the cost reduces over time.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the blurred image buffer, $O(H \cdot W \cdot C)$ [G].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On DTU 3-view, Smoothed 2D Supervision (as part of Adaptive Gaussian Blur) achieves 19.72 PSNR, improving over the baseline of 19.54 PSNR [S3].
-*   **Strengths:** Stabilizes camera alignment, restricts high-frequency noise, and reduces training iterations required for convergence (e.g., 50k vs 200k in some MLP-based methods) [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Handcrafted schedule may not fully align frequency relationships compared to adaptive methods [S3]. Requires careful tuning of the schedule parameters [S4].
-*   **When to Use:** Crucial for successful pose refinement in joint optimization scenarios [S1]. Useful when training is unstable or prone to local minima.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When high-frequency detail preservation is critical from the start, or when computational resources are extremely limited and the overhead of blurring is prohibitive.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Adds a convolution operation per training iteration per view. The cost is proportional to $O(W \cdot H \cdot K^2)$ per image, where $W, H$ are image dimensions and $K$ is kernel size. Since $K$ decreases over time, the cost is highest in early iterations [S4].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the blurred image buffer, adding $O(W \cdot H)$ memory per view [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On DTU 3-view, Smoothed 2D Supervision (specifically the "Adaptive Gaussian Blur" variant) achieves 19.72 PSNR, improving over the Baseline of 19.54 PSNR [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Stabilizes camera alignment and prevents overfitting to high-frequency noise during early training [S2, unofficial]. It is experimentally proven crucial for successful pose refinement [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The schedule is often handcrafted and may not fully align frequency relationships between positional encodings and pixel supervision as effectively as adaptive methods [S3].
+*   **When to Use:** Use when joint optimization of camera poses and scene geometry is unstable or when training converges to noisy, high-frequency artifacts. It is particularly effective in early-stage training [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Smoothed 2D Supervision was introduced as part of techniques to improve the robustness of joint optimization for camera pose and radiance fields [S1][S2, unofficial]. It draws inspiration from spectral analysis of rendering losses, noting that blurring training images produces smoother gradients that stabilize optimization [S2, unofficial]. It is often compared to other regularization methods like Linear frequency weighting and Adaptive Rendering Loss, with which it shares the goal of stabilizing training but differs in the mechanism of applying spectral constraints [S3].
+Smoothed 2D Supervision was introduced to improve the robustness of joint optimization in radiance field reconstruction. It draws inspiration from spectral analysis suggesting that blurring training images produces smoother gradients, which stabilizes camera alignment [S2, unofficial]. The technique was notably applied in methods like Trick-GS for Gaussian Splatting and in NeRF-based approaches for adaptive rendering loss regularization [S3][S4]. It addresses the inconsistency between the frequency regularization of positional encodings and the raw pixel supervision [S3].
 
